{"parentUuid":"586a072f-01f4-4640-9088-bb46f25aba73","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"4d2f9563-2eb9-4fc2-ac2d-83a7c53a99bd","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UP1dHPgKBxBC4WGKNeuKto","type":"tool_result","content":"| D1 | Rust for all Bastion code. Another language only where no Rust path exists, named here with its reason: `.pi/e\n| D4 | One dark theme, purple as the main color. Its tokens live in `crates/bastion-desktop/src/theme.rs`. \n| D6 | Every Bastion worker is a Rust binary on `iii-sdk` 0.25.0, started by compose through `scripts.run`. \n| D7 | A stock iii worker comes first. Bastion writes a worker only for what no pinned worker does. \n| D8 | Records live in the `database` worker as typed columns. Each row carries a `rev`, and a stale write is refused\n| D10 | Updates are pushed, never polled: the server observes the engine, and clients follow the server's events (D29)\n| D13 | The local server starts the stack (`iii compose`) and attaches to one that already runs. The stack goes down w\n| D14 | Every agent turn runs in an `iii-sandbox` VM on its job's worktree. No credential enters an agent's VM. A shel\n| D16 | A note on a step goes to the agent word for word as its next brief. A fix loop stops after a set count, or soo\n| D17 | Every job runs a workflow. A free-form task is the shipped Short workflow, not a second mode. \n| D18 | The rules a job runs by live in `bastion-core`: pure functions from an event and a state to a new state and ef\n| D19 | The seams are Cargo dependencies, and `scripts/check-seams.sh` fails CI when one is crossed. Only the engine a\n| D20 | Every constraint and capability is enforced in a hook, and its rung is data, so an eval can name what changed.\n| D21 | The audit record is a hash chain with one writer. Each entry carries an HMAC-SHA256 of its fields and the entr\n| D22 | An eval compares two harness configurations on the same requests from the same commit, and reports the differe\n| D23 | The tracker, model providers and notifications are adapters behind seams, chosen in Settings. Pull requests, C\n| D25 | Every rule belongs to one layer: the charter (the repository's own), the harness (an agent's calls), or the fa\n| D26 | Rules, grants and settings are data in the server's database, versioned, edited on the pages; first-run defaul\n| D27 | The server is the source of truth and holds all business logic. A client shows state and sends a person's acti\n| D28 | The API is HTTP with JSON, one route per action on one resource, typed in `bastion-protocol`, with a bearer to\n| D29 | Push is server-sent events on `GET /events`, with ids. A client that reconnects with `Last-Event-ID` gets what\n| D30 | A client keeps a list of servers, each a name, a URL and a token in the OS keychain. `local` is the server on \n| D31 | A server has people with roles: an admin manages it, a member submits and acts on their own jobs, a viewer rea\n| D32 | A server listens on loopback unless it has a TLS certificate and key. \n| D33 | Each step is lights-on or dark. A workflow's level follows from its steps. No step waits on a timer. Every dec\n| D34 | Model provider keys live in the stack's vault, read only by `llm-router`, never in the environment and never r\n| D35 | A workflow is a graph of steps a person edits on a canvas. Steps pass declared results to the steps linked aft\n| D36 | Claude takes an API key only. No Claude subscription sign-in. \n| D37 | The desktop client is a native Rust app on `iced`. It holds no job or workflow logic and reaches a server only\n| D38 | Every client goes through `crates/bastion-client`: the server list, typed calls, and the event stream. A new i\n| D39 | A workflow is a series of steps, and nothing else runs. A step is a prompt, an agent (`.agents/agents`), a ski\n| D40 | A shell step runs in the job's VM, with no network unless it asks. A step that asks gets a VM of its own with \n| D41 | A job ends with its branch ready. When the last step passes, a workspace set to merge merges the branch into i\n| D42 | Governance is per step. A step may change files or only reads, and the gate refuses a write from a step that o\n| D43 | A step may name its own model and budget. The model wins over the job's and the server's. The budget is how of\n| D44 | A step is known as `workflow/step`. A grant may be for one workflow step, and it wins over the grant for its k\n| D45 | A step is a prompt, an agent, a skill or a shell script. A repository's command is not a kind of its own: a pr\n| D46 | A repository's charter is `AGENTS.md` and `.agents/`: `.agents/settings.json` holds its permissions and hooks,\n| D47 | A link may be a failure link: when its step fails, the job goes along it instead of back to the step that writ\n| D48 | A grant is ask or allowed; a call no grant covers is refused, so there is no denied grant. Demoting a grant fr\n| D49 | A grant on `sandbox::exec` may name a command, and then it covers only a line that starts with those words and\n| D50 | A constraint may be a hook: a command that runs in the job's VM before each matching call at its steps, after \n| D51 | A constraint may name a reviewer from `.agents/agents/`. When its step finishes, the reviewer reads the step's\n| D52 | A server's MCP servers reach a turn through `workers/bastion-mcp`, which registers each tool as `mcp::<server>\n| D53 | A step's rules live with the step, on the Workflows page, in one tab by layer: the charter (read-only, from th\n| D54 | A constraint or a grant is one item that three scopes turn on or off: all workflows (`*`), one workflow, and o\n| D55 | A constraint is judged by an agent (a told rule, a reviewer) or by code (a word match, a hook, a review check)\n| D56 | A constraint or capability is feedforward, which guides the agent before it acts (a rule it is told, a skill, \n| D57 | Rules live on three pages, Constraints, Capabilities and Permissions, each with a Global view and a Workflows \n| D58 | Noth