{"parentUuid":"160a28ec-8397-4d2c-81d0-7f91c434d739","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"c05ae742-b7ea-4081-ac5f-2b7df3580a90","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01H9iVDgzS3KRSW3T1K1zHcU","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t<!-- voice-register: argument -->\n2\t\n3\t# The software expedition\n4\t\n5\tSay \"software factory\" to a room of engineers and watch half of them wince. Some see building software as a craft, and nobody likes hearing their craft is an assembly line. Others hear waterfall wearing a hard hat. And a few remember that a factory is already a design pattern. So now the word means two things, and neither is fun at parties.\n6\t\n7\tI'll admit it up front: software factories can work. I've [built one with the lights on](https://blog.tacoda.dev/build-your-software-factory-with-the-lights-on-bb42437261a5), and I'd build it again. But factories aren't foolproof, plenty of work doesn't fit on a line, and many good engineers reject the whole premise. That's fair. So instead of arguing, let me offer a different picture.\n8\t\n9\tLet's call it the software expedition. It's a label about outcomes and value instead of PR volume, and it's on agile's side instead of waterfall's. It isn't a new method, and you don't have to learn any new words. It's a change of perspective. Treat your product like a field study, where the job is finding out what's true, not producing as much as possible.\n10\t\n11\t![An old-world map of a software expedition, from the outpost past base camp, the charter, the checkpoint, and the guide, up through the range of reality to the summit, with the return to the atlas and the next region visible beyond](diagrams/expedition-chart.png)\n12\t*One trip, from the outpost to the summit and home again. Mind the sea serpent: here be scope creep.*\n13\t\n14\t## Why go exploring\n15\t\n16\tA production line is proud of how much comes out the other end. That made sense when writing code was the slow part. Now agents can write a lot of code very fast, so producing is cheap, and overproducing is the easy mistake. You can ship a lovely feature nobody needed before anyone stops to ask.\n17\t\n18\tExplorers had a different job. Nobody sailed off to manufacture a jungle. They went to see what was out there, and they came back with specimens and field notes. Your work can run the same way. Every piece of work is a question about the product. The change is how we ask it, and what comes back says where to go next.\n19\t\n20\tAnd that points us at the question we've been trying to answer all along. What's the next valuable piece of work? What will have the most outsized impact? What outcome do we actually want? A production line can't tell you, because all it can see is the queue.\n21\t\n22\t## Reading the map\n23\t\n24\tFollow the trail and you'll recognize every stop, because your team already does all of it.\n25\t\n26\tWe start at the outpost, where word arrives that something needs exploring: a request, a bug, or a page at an hour nobody likes. We pick the summit before we leave, and the first thing it has to be is that it works. The harness is our guide, the thing that gets us up the mountain. These days that's the agent harness, with its models, its tools, and its loop.\n27\t\n28\tWe set up base camp somewhere safe, like a branch or a sandbox. We carry the charter, which is how we work and how we check. And every so often we reach a checkpoint, a known good state to fall back to if the weather turns.\n29\t\n30\tThen reality pushes back. The range of reality is every peak between us and the summit: failing tests, red CI, a support ticket, UAT sent back. Each one is feedback. Each one also leads up to the final peak, which is the boring, wonderful news that it actually works.\n31\t\n32\tOnly from up there can we see the next region worth surveying. So we head home with specimens (the change we shipped) and field notes (what reality told us, signed by a person). Some trails end in the marsh, where the work went nowhere. That's fine, as long as we say so. It all goes into the atlas the team keeps, so the next trip starts from what we learned instead of a guess.\n33\t\n34\t![A field guide to the map: each landmark, what happens there, and what it is in software](diagrams/expedition-map.png)\n35\t*The field guide, for anyone who likes a legend.*\n36\t\n37\t## \"But exploring never ends\"\n38\t\n39\tProduct will push back here, and fairly. Exploration sounds like a trip with no plan and no deadline, and nobody wants to fund a jungle cruise.\n40\t\n41\tBut every real expedition had a destination, rules for when to turn around, and supplies that ran out. Software work has the same: an outcome to reach, a way to tell whether it got there, and a budget of time, tokens, or tries. When the budget's gone, the team comes home and reports what it found, even if the answer is \"not this.\" It's bounded like any other work. It just doesn't pretend to know the territory before it gets there.\n42\t\n43\t## Try it on your last trip\n44\t\n45\tTake the last bug fix, feature, or page your team handled, and tell it as an expedition. What sent you out? Where was the summit, and did you reach it? What did you bring back besides the change? Then write the field note it should have left behind, in one sentence. If that sentence points at the next piece of work, congratulations: you've been exploring all along.\n46\t\n47\tAll of this is a mental model, and I hope it helps. If it doesn't, take one thing from this post: stop doing production, and start doing exploration that produces valuable outcomes.\n48\t"}]},"uuid":"d11b6dbc-d590-46f9-9511-81beeb472d45","timestamp":"2026-11-12T21:11:37.279Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/all-the-things/drafts/ready/software-expedition.md","content":"<!-- voice-register: argument -->\n\n# The software expedition\n\nSay \"software factory\" to a room of engineers and watch half of them wince. Some see building software as a craft, and nobody likes hearing their craft is an assembly line. Others hear waterfall wearing a hard hat. And a few remember that a factory is already a design pattern. So now the word means two things, and neither is fun at parties.\n\nI'll admit it up front: software factories can work. I've [built one with the lights on](https://blog.tacoda.dev/build-your-software-factory-with-the-lights-on-bb42437261a5), and I'd build it again. But factories aren't foolproof, plenty of work doesn't fit on a line, and many good engineers reject the whole premise. That's fair. So instead of arguing, let me offer a different picture.\n\nLet's call it the software expedition. It's a label about outcomes and value instead of PR volume, and it's on agile's side instead of waterfall's. It isn't a new method, and you don't have to learn any new words. It's a change of perspective. Treat your product like a field study, where the job is finding out what's true, not producing as much as possible.\n\n![An old-world map of a software expedition, from the outpost past base camp, the charter, the checkpoint, and the guide, up through the range of reality to the summit, with the return to the atlas and the next region visible beyond](diagrams/expedition-chart.png)\n*One trip, from the outpost to the summit and home again. Mind the sea serpent: here be scope creep.*\n\n## Why go exploring\n\nA production line is proud of how much comes out the other end. That made sense when writing code was the slow part. Now agents can write a lot of code very fast, so producing is cheap, and overproducing is the easy mistake. You can ship a lovely feature nobody needed before anyone stops to ask.\n\nExplorers had a different job. Nobody sailed off to manufacture a jungle. They went to see what was out there, and they came back with specimens and field notes. Your work can run the same way. Every piece of work is a question about the product. The change is how we ask it, and what comes back says where to go next.\n\nAnd that points us at the question we've been trying to answer all along. What's the next valuable piece of work? What will have the most outsized impact? What outcome do we actually want? A production line can't tell you, because all it can see is the queue.\n\n## Reading the map\n\nFollow the trail and you'll recognize every stop, because your team already does all of it.\n\nWe start at the outpost, where word arrives that something needs exploring: a request, a bug, or a page at an hour nobody likes. We pick the summit before we leave, and the first thing it has to be is that it works. The harness is our guide, the thing that gets us up the mountain. These days that's the agent harness, with its models, its tools, and its loop.\n\nWe set up base camp somewhere safe, like a branch or a sandbox. We carry the charter, which is how we work and how we check. And every so often we reach a checkpoint, a known good state to fall back to if the weather turns.\n\nThen reality pushes back. The range of reality is every peak between us and the summit: failing tests, red CI, a support ticket, UAT sent back. Each one is feedback. Each one also leads up to the final peak, which is the boring, wonderful news that it actually works.\n\nOnly from up there can we see the next region worth surveying. So we head home with specimens (the change we shipped) and field notes (what reality told us, signed by a person). Some trails end in the marsh, where the work went nowhere. That's fine, as long as we say so. It all goes into the atlas the team keeps, so the next trip starts from what we learned instead of a guess.\n\n![A field guide to the map: each landmark, what happens there, and what it is in software](diagrams/expedition-map.png)\n*The field guide, for anyone who likes a legend.*\n\n## \"But exploring never ends\"\n\nProduct will push back here, and fairly. Exploration sounds like a trip with no plan and no deadline, and nobody wants to fund a jungle cruise.\n\nBut every real expedition had a destination, rules for when to turn around, and supplies that ran out. Software work has the same: an outcome to reach, a way to tell whether it got there, and a budget of time, tokens, or tries. When the budget's gone, the team comes home and reports what it found, even if the answer is \"not this.\" It's bounded like any other work. It just doesn't pretend to know the territory before it gets there.\n\n## Try it on your last trip\n\nTake the last bug fix, feature, or page your team handled, and tell it as an expedition. What sent you out? Where was the summit, and did you reach it? What did you bring back besides the change? Then write the field note it should have left behind, in one sentence. If that sentence points at the next piece of work, congratulations: you've been exploring all along.\n\nAll of this is a mental model, and I hope it helps. If it doesn't, take one thing from this post: stop doing production, and start doing exploration that produces valuable outcomes.\n","numLines":48,"startLine":1,"totalLines":48}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"160a28ec-8397-4d2c-81d0-7f91c434d739","session_id":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"main","slug":"parsed-tinkering-blum"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-12T21:11:37.539Z","sessionId":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","content":"The main differentiator is the ability to define multiple workflows - I don't know of any other factories that allow that level of control - this is something bigger - an orchestration control plane"}
